---
import Badge from './ui/Badge.astro'
import s from './Why.module.css'

// Section two, the reference's two showcase panels: one run accounted for, and an honest look at the neighbours.
// Every neighbour claim is from that project's own docs (linked under the caption), checked 19 Nov 2026.
const rows = [
  { name: 'Node.js', text: 'Everything. --permission is opt-in.' },
  { name: 'Bun', text: 'Everything.' },
  { name: 'Deno', text: 'Nothing until a flag grants it.' },
  { name: 'jsrt', text: 'Nothing until the manifest grants it.', us: true },
]
---

<section class="section" id="why">
  <div class={`container ${s.wrap}`}>
    <header class={s.head}>
      <Badge>Why jsrt</Badge>
      <h2 class="t-h2">Built for code you don't fully trust</h2>
    </header>
    <div class={s.grid}>
      <figure class={s.item}>
        <div class={`glow ${s.panel}`} style="--gx: 0%; --gy: 100%;">
          <div class={`float ${s.card}`}>
            <p class={s.cardHead}>
              <span class={s.mark} aria-hidden="true"></span>
              <span class="mono">webhook.js</span>
            </p>
            <dl class={s.facts}>
              <div><dt>Status</dt><dd><span class={s.ok}>Classified</span></dd></div>
              <div><dt>Exit code</dt><dd class="mono">7 · capability</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Manifest</dt><dd class="mono">net: app.example.com:443</dd></div>
            </dl>
            <p class={s.sub}>Events</p>
            <ol class={s.timeline}>
              <li><span class="mono">rt.fetch</span><span>api.other.com refused</span></li>
              <li><span class="mono">exit 7</span><span>reported, never a crash</span></li>
            </ol>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption class={s.caption}><strong>A refusal has a name.</strong> The script can catch the CapabilityError; uncaught, the shell sees exit 7, never a stack trace from a crash.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class={s.item}>
        <div class={`glow ${s.panel}`} style="--gx: 100%; --gy: 100%;">
          <div class={`float ${s.card}`}>
            <p class={s.cardHead}>
              <span class={s.cardTitle}>Default access to the host</span>
            </p>
            <ul class={s.table}>
              {
                rows.map((r) => (
                  <li class={r.us ? s.us : ''}>
                    <span class={s.who}>{r.name}</span>
                    <span>{r.text}</span>
                  </li>
                ))
              }
            </ul>
            <p class={s.note}><span class="mono">npm:</span> and <span class="mono">node:</span> modules: Node, Deno and Bun run them; jsrt refuses them at resolve time, by design.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption class={s.caption}>
          <strong>Honest about the neighbours.</strong> Node's docs call its permission model a seatbelt, not a security boundary, and Deno denies by default too. jsrt adds a manifest per run, a fixed exit contract and a supervised process, and gives up npm to do it.
          <span class={s.sources}>Sources: <a class="inline-link" href="https://nodejs.org/api/permissions.html">Node permissions</a>, <a class="inline-link" href="https://docs.deno.com/runtime/fundamentals/security/">Deno security</a>.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
